From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 17:18:41 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(allwinner): A733: DSU cluster OFF at PSCI power level 1

Second step of the A733 cluster idle state, on top of "every core
requires DSU cluster power" (every core sets its CLUSTERPWRDN_EL1
"cluster power required" bit whenever it comes up).

WHAT. At cold boot the DSU PPU (0x07051000) policy floor comes down from
ON to OFF: PWR_POLICY [3:0] = 0, PWR_DYN_EN and OP_POLICY kept (Arm
DEN0051E 5.2.1, read-modify-write of the nibble, read back and printed).
CPU_SUSPEND then also accepts 0x01010000 (POWERDOWN, level 1, StateID
0), while the PPU still reads policy OFF with dynamic transitions on. A
core that asks for level 1, and a core on CPU_OFF, writes
CLUSTERPWRDN_EL1 = 0; every core writes 1 again when it comes up. The
DSU powers the cluster down only when all eight cores are down and every
bit is 0 (DSU TRM 100453 B1.14/B1.15); the requested level decides the
bit, not the coordinated one, because the DSU combines the bits itself.

THE GIC. A cluster power-down on this SoC resets every core's GIC-600
redistributor SGI/PPI configuration (A733 UM V1.00 5.1.9.17, C0_RST_CTRL
bit 4 GIC_RST, the cluster's internal GIC reset), while GICR_PWRR still
reads the redistributor powered. Writing the saved configuration back
alone leaves PPI 26 pending and enabled and never delivered: Linux loses
its tick on the first power-down. It takes a GICR_PWRR power transition
around the loss, done here in the GIC-600 TRM (100336 r1p6) order: after
gicv3_cpuif_disable() (3.6.2: ProcessorSleep set, ChildrenAsleep polled;
the generic GIC driver runs it before pwr_domain_suspend) a core that
asked for level 1 saves its SGI/PPI frame and writes GICR_PWRR.RDPD = 1
(3.6.1; Table 4-26: only accepted with ProcessorSleep = 1); at wake, in
pwr_domain_suspend_finish and so before the generic driver re-enables
the CPU interface, RDPD = 0 and the frame is written back if it reads
different, in the order of TF-A's gicv3_rdistif_init_restore() (SGIs and
PPIs disabled, RWP, configuration, enables). gicv3_rdistif_init() is not
needed. Measured on the A733: init alone hangs on the first power-down;
this order ran 3235 cluster power-downs in 120 s with one core, and with
the cluster held ON the same power-off keeps the frame and every wake
works. TF-A's FVP comment warns against this order ("can potentially
prevent wake up events"); measured, it does not here.

The redistributor frames are the ones the generic GIC driver found
(gicv3_rdistif_get_base()). Each core checks once that its frame is
where the GIC-600 register map puts it (TRM Table 4-1:
SUNXI_GICR_BASE + core * 128 KiB) and ends in ERROR and panic if not:
the boot core at cold boot, every other core after CPU_ON, in
pwr_domain_on_finish_late, once the driver has probed its frame. The
GICR_CTLR.RWP waits poll without a bound, as the GIC driver's own
gicr_wait_for_pending_write(): the GICv3 architecture gives RWP no time
bound (IHI 0069H.b 12.11.2), and a deadline from timeout_init_us() is
not available there. It reads CNTFRQ_EL0, which the core's power-down
reset and which the PSCI library programs again only after
pwr_domain_suspend_finish (lib/psci/psci_suspend.c).

THE L3. No software maintenance: the DSU cleans the L3 when the cluster
is asked to power down (DSU TRM A4.9, A5.1). Measured: 512 KiB of dirty
lines read back complete through a non-cacheable alias after every
window with a cluster power-down, while a control without one still read
them dirty.

WHAT THE VENDOR DOES. Nothing to port: the vendor ARISC firmware takes
the cluster down only for system suspend and never writes the DSU
policy, CLUSTERPWRDN or the GIC; its BL31 is a blob. Its device tree
offers cluster-sleep-0 (0x1010000) on all cores with its own latencies,
which are not used.

Build switch SUNXI_DSU_CLUSTER_OFF, default 0, 1 for the A733. Without a
level-1 idle state in the device tree nothing asks for it, and the
cluster cannot power down: a core in the core-level state keeps its bit
at 1.

Assisted-by: Claude
